## 8. Final Analysis

### Why DLRM differs from the vanilla neural network

The vanilla neural network learns feature relationships implicitly
through its dense layers.

DLRM explicitly represents categorical features using embeddings and
calculates pairwise dot-product interactions between the feature
representations before passing them to the top MLP.

For this implementation:

- 26 categorical features
- 1 numerical representation
- 27 total feature vectors
- 351 pairwise interactions
- 16-dimensional embeddings

The explicit interaction design is particularly useful for
recommendation problems where combinations of categorical features
can carry information about user behaviour.

### DLRM vs Matrix Factorization

Matrix Factorization from Task 01 learns latent representations
mainly for users and items and uses their interaction to predict
ratings.

DLRM is designed for a richer CTR setting with many categorical and
numerical fields. It can represent multiple feature types and
explicitly model interactions between them.

### Computational considerations

DLRM requires embedding tables for the categorical features and
computes pairwise interactions between feature representations.

For 27 feature vectors, this implementation calculates 351 pairwise
interactions for every example.

The embedding tables also contribute significantly to model memory,
especially when categorical features have high cardinality.

### Overall conclusion

The experiments show that model architecture and regularization both
matter for CTR prediction.

The regularized vanilla neural network (NN-2) achieved the strongest
validation metrics in this experiment. DLRM provided a more
structured way to represent categorical feature interactions and
performed better than the original unregularized neural network.

However, the ablation indicates that explicit interactions did not
provide a clear performance improvement on this particular dataset.
This highlights that architectural complexity does not necessarily
translate into better validation performance for every dataset.
